From: Rob Watson <rob@mediaeden.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/a3xx: forget the loaded context when the GPU is reset

a3xx_submit() skips a submit's context-restore buffer when the context has
not changed since the last submit:

	case MSM_SUBMIT_CMD_CTX_RESTORE_BUF:
		/* ignore if there has not been a ctx switch: */
		if (ring->cur_ctx_seqno == submit->queue->ctx->seqno)
			break;

That is correct while the GPU keeps running, because the state the restore
buffer would load is already in the hardware. It stops being correct the
moment the GPU is reset: recovery takes the device through
pm_runtime_get_sync(), which sets needs_hw_init, and hw_init() then brings the
hardware back up with none of that state. ring->cur_ctx_seqno still names the
context that was loaded before the reset, so the next submit from that same
context skips the restore it now needs and runs against a GPU that has
forgotten everything.

a6xx clears it in its hw_init() for exactly this reason, and has since
per-ringbuffer contexts were introduced:

	for (i = 0; i < gpu->nr_rings; i++)
		gpu->rb[i]->cur_ctx_seqno = 0;

a3xx and a4xx never clear it anywhere.

The bug needs two GPU clients to show itself. With one client the context
never changes, so the restore is skipped on every submit regardless and the
stale value costs nothing. With two -- a Wayland compositor and an
application, which alternate submits -- the restore matters on every submit,
and after the first recovery the wrong one is skipped.

That matches what this phone does. An application rendering with GL alongside
a GL compositor faults within seconds and the faults arrive in bursts; the
same application against a compositor on its software renderer, so that only
one process uses the GPU, runs indefinitely with none. Measured on an Xperia
Z2 (MSM8974PRO-AB, Adreno 330):

	compositor GLES2 + app cairo     faults 0
	compositor GLES2 + app opengl    faults 3, then 4
	compositor pixman + app opengl   faults 0, then 0

This does not explain the first fault in a burst -- nothing has been reset at
that point -- but it does explain why one fault turns into several, and why a
GPU reset on this part so often takes the session with it.

Signed-off-by: Rob Watson <rob@mediaeden.com>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a3xx_gpu.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a3xx_gpu.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a3xx_gpu.c
@@ -320,6 +320,15 @@
 	/* clear ME_HALT to start micro engine */
 	gpu_write(gpu, REG_AXXX_CP_ME_CNTL, 0);
 
+	/*
+	 * The hardware has just come up with no context loaded, so forget
+	 * whichever one a3xx_submit() last saw. Without this the next submit
+	 * from that context skips its restore buffer and runs against a GPU
+	 * that was reset out from under it.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < gpu->nr_rings; i++)
+		gpu->rb[i]->cur_ctx_seqno = 0;
+
 	return a3xx_me_init(gpu) ? 0 : -EINVAL;
 }
 
